    "D",
    "E",
    "F"
  ],
  "correct": "D",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Eliminate one variable",
      "body": "<p>The linear equation gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Substituting this into the other equation leaves an equation involving only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "A linear relationship can remove one variable from a simultaneous pair.",
      "pitfall": "Do not replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>: it is a product.",
      "pause": "Substitute for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and multiply the whole equation by 3.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-simultaneous-equations",
          "reminder": "Substitute the expression from the linear equation into the quadratic equation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Use the quadratic root sum",
      "body": "Expand and collect the substituted equation. Once it is quadratic, its root sum can be obtained from the linear and leading coefficients without finding each root separately.",
      "recap": "For a quadratic, the root sum is the negative linear coefficient divided by the leading coefficient.",
      "pitfall": "Check that the substituted equation remains equivalent to the original pair.",
      "pause": "Derive the quadratic yourself and calculate the sum of its two roots.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l02",
          "reminder": "For a quadratic, the root sum is the negative linear coefficient divided by the leading coefficient."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Substitution in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> and multiplication by 3 give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>This factors as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>. Each determines a valid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> through the linear equation. Their sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3.5</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>Answer D.</strong> Equivalently, read the sum directly as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
